        "bodyTr": "Yesterday, the KOSPI index plummeted 7.57 percent, breaking through the 5,600 level. During intraday trading, both the KOSPI and KOSDAQ indices fell more than 8 percent in a panic-driven market, triggering circuit breakers that halted stock trading. This marks the first time circuit breakers have been activated in both markets since July 29. Stock prices collapsed amid renewed concerns about additional interest rate hikes in the United States and resurgent talk of artificial intelligence investment reaching its peak. While major Asian stock markets including Japan (down 4.9 percent) and Taiwan (down 3.41 percent) fell in tandem, South Korea's stock market decline was notably steeper. Experts attribute this partly to the market's heavy dependence on memory semiconductors, but more significantly to a breakdown in supply and demand dynamics in the stock market. Foreign investors have been dumping stocks on a massive scale, yet there is no meaningful buying force to absorb these sales. What is most regrettable in this situation is the absence of the National Pension Service's role. The National Pension Service, a major market player, has historically performed well as a firefighter during market crashes. It has done so through rebalancing—selling when stocks are expensive and buying when they are cheap, while adjusting investment allocations accordingly. While this strategy is intended to pursue the long-term profitability and stability of pensions, it has naturally served as an automatic safety mechanism for the stock market. However, in recent market crashes, the National Pension Service and other pension funds have been virtually invisible. Yesterday, while foreign investors net-sold 3.6638 trillion won, pension funds managed only 350 billion won in net purchases. The National Pension Service's hands have been tied because it failed to reduce its domestic stock allocation during the recent market surge. After the KOSPI index surged in the latter half of last year, the National Pension Service's asset management committee postponed rebalancing in January of this year and substantially increased its domestic stock investment ratio in May. This extraordinary departure from principle sparked concerns among experts, and civic groups including the two major labor federations issued statements expressing worry about compromised fund management independence. Overseas investment banks even pointed out that the National Pension Service had become an amplifier of volatility rather than a market stabilizer. At the time, when the National Pension Service refrained from selling stocks, retail investors cheered and the government was relieved of political burden. However, as this market crash has demonstrated, there is no free lunch in economics, and every choice comes with a price. Particularly when the price is paid for choices that violate principles, the cost can only be severe.",
